text:
Template Usage
Next, you define your main HTML (Jinja2) templates as usual. Then define your partial templates. I recommend locating and naming them accordingly:
├── templates
│   ├── home
│   │   ├── index.html
│   │   └── listing.html
│   └── shared
│       ├── _layout.html
│       └── partials
│           ├── video_image.html
│           └── video_square.html
Notice the partials subfolder in the templates/shared folder.
The templates are just HTML fragments. Here is a stand-alone one for the YouTube thumbnail from the example app:
&lt;img src="https://img.youtube.com/vi/{{ video.id }}/maxresdefault.jpg"
     class="img img-responsive {{ (classes | default([])) | join(' ') }}"
     alt="{{ video.title }}"
     title="{{ video.title }}"&gt;
Notice that an object called video is passed in as the model, along with an optional list of CSS classes.
Templates can also be nested. Here is the whole single video fragment with the image as well as other info linking out to YouTube:
&lt;div&gt;
    &lt;a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v={{ video.id }}" target="_blank"&gt;
        {{ render_partial('shared/partials/video_image.html', video=video) }}
    &lt;/a&gt;
    &lt;a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v={{ video.id }}" target="_blank"
       class="author"&gt;{{ video.author }}&lt;/a&gt;
    &lt;div class="views"&gt;{{ "{:,}".format(video.views) }} views&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
Now you see the render_partial() method. It takes the subpath into the templates folder and any model data passed in as keyword arguments.
We can finally generate the list of video blocks as follows:
{% for v in videos %}

    &lt;div class="col-md-3 video"&gt;
        {{ render_partial('shared/partials/video_square.html', video=v) }}
    &lt;/div&gt;

{% endfor %}
This time, we reframe each item in the list from the outer template (called v) as the video model in the inner HTML section.
If a fragment shows up on the page as escaped text, or you hit PartialsException, TemplateNotFound, or an asyncio.run() error in an async app, the Troubleshooting page lists the cause and fix for each.

This page builds the smallest useful example with Flask. Every other framework differs only in the registration line, covered in Frameworks.

1. Register once at startup
# app.py
import flask
import jinja_partials

app = flask.Flask(__name__)
jinja_partials.register_extensions(app)


@app.get('/')
def index():
    return flask.render_template('home/index.html', videos=get_videos())
Registration installs render_partial as a global in the app’s Jinja environment. Do it once, when the app is built, before it starts serving.


2. Lay out the templates
Partials are ordinary templates. The convention this project uses is a partials folder under templates/shared:
templates/
├── home/
│   └── index.html
└── shared/
    ├── _layout.html
    └── partials/
        ├── video_image.html
        └── video_square.html
Template names passed to render_partial are paths relative to the templates root, with forward slashes and the file extension, exactly like the names you pass to render_template.


3. Write the partials
The innermost fragment renders a YouTube thumbnail. It receives a video object and an optional list of CSS classes:
&lt;!-- templates/shared/partials/video_image.html --&gt;
&lt;img src="https://img.youtube.com/vi/{{ video.id }}/maxresdefault.jpg"
     class="img img-responsive {{ (classes | default([])) | join(' ') }}"
     alt="{{ video.title }}"
     title="{{ video.title }}"&gt;
Partials nest. The video card renders the image partial inside itself:
&lt;!-- templates/shared/partials/video_square.html --&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
    &lt;a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v={{ video.id }}" target="_blank"&gt;
        {{ render_partial('shared/partials/video_image.html', video=video) }}
    &lt;/a&gt;
    &lt;a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v={{ video.id }}" target="_blank"
       class="author"&gt;{{ video.author }}&lt;/a&gt;
    &lt;div class="views"&gt;{{ "{:,}".format(video.views) }} views&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;


4. Use them from a page
&lt;!-- templates/home/index.html --&gt;
{% extends "shared/_layout.html" %}
{% block main_content %}
    {% for v in videos %}
        &lt;div class="col-md-3 video"&gt;
            {{ render_partial('shared/partials/video_square.html', video=v) }}
        &lt;/div&gt;
    {% endfor %}
{% endblock %}
The loop variable is v in the page and video inside the partial. That renaming is the normal way to use partials: the fragment declares what it needs, and the caller maps its own data onto those names.


5. What you should see
Run the app and open the page. Each video renders as a card like this, with the image fragment inlined where the nested render_partial call was:
&lt;div class="col-md-3 video"&gt;
    &lt;div&gt;
        &lt;a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=abc123" target="_blank"&gt;
            &lt;img src="https://img.youtube.com/vi/abc123/maxresdefault.jpg" class="img img-responsive " alt="Intro to Flask" title="Intro to Flask"&gt;
        &lt;/a&gt;
        &lt;a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=abc123" target="_blank" class="author"&gt;Talk Python&lt;/a&gt;
        &lt;div class="views"&gt;12,500 views&lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
If instead you see the markup itself on the page as text (&lt;div&gt; and friends), the partial was inserted as an escaped string. That happens on the plain Jinja2 path when markup=True was not passed. Troubleshooting covers that and the other errors you can hit.
The full version of this app, with the layout, CSS, and a small JSON database, is the Flask example in the repository.
